d_at) |\n| Hierarchy | Yes (`parent` self-FK) | No |\n| `content_type` | Yes (FK to ContentType) | No |\n| AI-specific fields | None | `param_semantics`, `filter_endpoint` |\n| Usage scope | 15+ apps across the project | Only `ai_assistant` |\n| Methods | Rich (get_descendants, get_full_path, display_data, etc.) | Minimal (only __str__) |\n\n**Analysis:**\n\nThe two models serve fundamentally different purposes:\n\n1. **core.EquipmentType** is a general-purpose hierarchical classifier of equipment types used across the entire project (cert docs, features, filters, media library, prices, valve data — 15+ apps). It ties equipment types to models via `content_type` and has a rich parent-child hierarchy.\n\n2. **ai_assistant.EquipmentType** is a lightweight lookup table that ONLY serves the AI pipeline. It has AI-specific fields (`param_semantics`, `filter_endpoint`) that core.EquipmentType doesn't have and shouldn't have.\n\nThe question is: should ai_assistant reuse core.EquipmentType? There are two approaches:\n\n**Option A: Merge into core.EquipmentType**\n- Add `param_semantics` and `filter_endpoint` to core.EquipmentType\n- Replace ai_assistant FK's (SelectionNode.equipment_type, StepConfig.equipment_type, CascadeRule.parent_type/child_type) to point at core.EquipmentType\n- Pros: single source of truth, no data duplication\n- Cons: pollutes the general model with AI-specific fields; core.EquipmentType is heavy (inherits 5 mixins, has hierarchy logic); might cause migration cascades across 15 apps\n\n**Option B: Create a FK link from ai_assistant.EquipmentType → core.EquipmentType**\n- Use ai_assistant.EquipmentType as an \"AI facade\" that links to the canonical type\n- Pros: keeps AI-specific metadata separate; ai_assistant can define its own subset of types with AI-relevant settings\n- Cons: still two tables, but now with a FK relationship\n\n**Option C: Keep separate (current state)**\n- Pros: zero changes, clean separation of concerns\n- Cons: potential data 